    preparation: "Do not consume any part. No part of larkspur is edible at any stage.",
    cautions: "TOXIC — can be fatal. All parts are poisonous, with the young spring leaves and the seeds the most alkaloid-rich — exactly the tender rosette stage a forager might mistake for edible greens. Ingestion causes burning of the mouth and throat, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea within hours, followed as the alkaloids are absorbed by muscle weakness and tremor, numbness, loss of coordination, a weak pulse, and — in serious cases — paralysis of the breathing muscles, convulsions and death. A California woman died after eating young larkspur she had gathered for a salad, mistaking it for wild mustard. Never eat unidentified spring greens; if ingested, call Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) at once.",
    toxicityNotes: "Contains numerous norditerpenoid alkaloids, chiefly the highly toxic methyllycaconitine (MLA), nudicauline and related compounds. These block muscle-type nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, producing neuromuscular paralysis; death is from respiratory failure. Alkaloid concentration is highest in the youngest growth and the seeds. Larkspurs kill more range cattle than any other poisonous plant, and human fatalities are documented.",
