    cautions: "Mechanical hazard, not poison: the plant is densely spiny - always de-spine and peel stems, midribs and root before eating. The taproot is rich in inulin, which humans do not digest and which can cause gas/bloating in some people; cooking helps. Make sure you actually have a thistle (spiny, composite flower heads) - there are no deadly thistle lookalikes, but avoid confusing rosettes with unrelated toxic rosette plants when not in flower.",
